  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B046",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B005",
    "B030"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged: B046 remains the strongest final public configuration. Optical lattice clocks meet a demanding experimental standard: independently reproduced 10^-18-level performance, controlled systematics, and use as working instruments for precision frequency comparison, relativity tests, and sensing. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock concept and its decisive experimental development provide a coherent, compact concept-to-realization achievement. The citation deliberately excludes optical frequency metrology in general; it recognizes the specific advance that made an ensemble of trapped neutral atoms a uniquely powerful clock.",
    "case_against": "The principal remaining issue is historical delimitation. We must verify that credit to Katori and Ye captures the invention and experimental establishment of lattice clocks without absorbing essential contributions from the larger optical-clock community. Downstream institutional milestones, including any redefinition of the second, should not be invoked as prerequisites or as part of the achievement.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that quantum error correction established the platform-independent physical basis for scalable quantum computation, although sustained fully fault-tolerant operation and exact credit remain unsettled.",
        "response": "I accept the conceptual depth of B010 and its importance for every quantum-information platform. My reservation remains experimental: the proposed citation concerns fault tolerance as a physical solution to decoherence, yet its decisive sustained realization is still incomplete. B046 instead joins a narrowly defined physical idea to repeatedly realized performance under stringent systematic control."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson emphasizes that quantized conductance in quantum point contacts is a direct, repeatedly reproduced experimental discovery, conditional on resolving parallel-discovery priority and complete credit.",
        "response": "I agree that B028 is exemplary direct experimental physics and that reproducibility is a major strength. The unresolved contemporaneous priority record, however, bears directly on whether its three-name attribution is complete. For B046, a similarly careful archival review is required, but the supplied concept-to-realization account currently permits a more sharply bounded citation and credit proposal."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson regards lattice clocks as the strongest competing case to inflation because they permit direct, controllable tests of relativity and metrology, while questioning whether their broader institutional adoption is fully settled.",
        "response": "I agree that adoption milestones should be distinguished from the scientific case. The mature result is not a prospective administrative change, but independently operating clocks whose comparisons already access a new precision regime. That demonstrated measurement capability is sufficient without claiming that every future application is established."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review supports credit limited to Katori and Ye for the specified lattice-clock achievement.",
      "How best to formulate the citation so that it excludes the wider history of optical frequency metrology while retaining both conceptual invention and experimental establishment."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict involving the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile. I note institutional overlap with Göran Johansson through WACQT and the broader Lund-Chalmers quantum-technology environment, but no specific collaboration or interest bearing on B046 is identified."
  }
}
